---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Work — Omnipeak"
  description="Case study: a ground-up rebuild of a booking and membership platform for a multi-location golf simulator business — the client's third attempt, and the one that shipped."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Case study</span>
      <h1>The booking platform that took three tries</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A ground-up rebuild of the reservation and membership system for a multi-location golf
        simulator business — after two previous developers failed to finish it.
      </p>
      <!-- CLIENT NAME SLOT: when permission is granted, name the business here and in the
           lede above, and add a logo/quote block after the intro. -->
      <ul class="case-meta">
        <li>Golf simulators</li>
        <li>Multi-location</li>
        <li>Reservations</li>
        <li>Memberships</li>
        <li>Stripe billing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The situation</h2>
        <p>
          The owners had paid for this system twice before. The first developer disappeared
          partway through. The second delivered something that never worked well enough to run
          the business on. By the time they contacted Omnipeak, they were running a
          multi-location operation on tools that couldn't handle their pricing, their
          memberships, or their growth plans — and they were understandably wary of hiring a
          third developer.
        </p>

        <h2>What was built</h2>
        <p>
          A complete booking and membership platform, rebuilt from the ground up rather than
          patched:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Reservations with real pricing rules.</strong> Simulator bays are booked in
            time slots, with pricing that changes by time of day — the way the business
            actually charges, enforced automatically instead of by staff.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Memberships and recurring billing.</strong> Member plans with recurring
            charges through Stripe, including the unglamorous parts: failed payments, plan
            changes, and cancellations, handled without manual work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Scheduled jobs.</strong> The routine work — billing runs, reminders,
            cleanup — happens on schedule, every time, with no one having to remember it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Built for more locations.</strong> The system is structured so new
            locations — including franchises — can be added without rebuilding anything.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <!-- OUTCOME NUMBERS SLOT: when real figures exist (bookings handled per month,
             revenue processed, staff hours saved), add a short stats row here. -->

        <h2>What the client got</h2>
        <p>
          A system their staff runs day to day through an admin interface built for
          non-technical operators — schedules, members, and pricing managed without calling
          anyone. The code, repository, hosting, and Stripe account are all in the client's
          name, with written documentation so any developer they ever hire can pick it up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The third attempt is the one the business now runs on.
        </p>
        <!-- QUOTE SLOT: client testimonial goes here when permission is granted. -->
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dim">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Does this sound familiar?</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        If you're on your second or third attempt at getting software built, the assessment
        comes first: an honest, written read on what you have before anything else is
        proposed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA title="Bring the project that stalled" />
</BaseLayout>
